1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:10,820
This video shows when an animation starts. It can start on load, on mouse over, or on click, and starting on load also waits until the animation can be seen.

2
00:00:11,367 --> 00:00:13,483
Let's add a ball that starts on load.

3
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:26,426
Start, set to load, plays it as soon as it is on the page.

4
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:30,018
Next, one that waits for the pointer.

5
00:00:37,767 --> 00:00:42,469
Start, set to mouse over, keeps it still until the pointer comes.

6
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:45,947
Let's point at it, and move away.

7
00:00:49,967 --> 00:00:52,161
It plays while the pointer is over it.

8
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,202
And one that starts on click.

9
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:07,980
Start, set to click, turns each click into play or pause.

10
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:10,584
Let's click it twice.

11
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,830
The first click plays it, and the second one pauses it.

12
00:01:19,067 --> 00:01:27,974
Starting on load has one more rule. It waits until the ball is half on screen. Let's add a fourth ball, far down the page.

13
00:01:40,033 --> 00:01:44,317
It also starts on load, but it is below the fold, so it has not started yet.

14
00:01:45,567 --> 00:01:46,716
Let's scroll down to it.

15
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:54,299
As soon as half of it is on screen, it starts.

16
00:01:55,533 --> 00:02:02,430
So, start can be load, mouse over or click, and a load start waits until the animation can be seen.
